I am of the opinion that Ranger and Knight are actually the easiest
classes to go out and ascend with.  ofc I play primarily slash'em
these days and I have a lot of nostalgia for orc ranger, as that was
my very fist ascention in nethack or slash'em (with my second being an
elven wizard)

Classes like Barbarian and Valkylrie may start with excellent starting
statistics, and a begining player will find that they are quite
capable in combat against *most* monsters.  But thats just the
problem- the aforementioned classes, unless they have a bit of luck
early on, don't have the tactical flexibility to change their battle
plan easily when they need to.

Rangers for instance start with their bow and +2 arrows.  They will
quickly accumulate daggers and then switch to those.  A Ranger can
fight weaker monsters by stabing them in close combat, they can deal
with medium monsters by throwing their stack of daggers at them, and
they can shoot down tougher monsters with +2 arrows.  This provides a
large variety of options, which all deal respectable damage, from up
to across the board.  The starting cloak of displacement, also allows
Rangers to more easily evade dangerous monsters.  I've encountered
several bones files where I've found a master mind flayer bones corpse
in the Mines.  a Ranger, especially one with expert in bow and
poisoned arrows, will be able to attack and kill the mind flayer where
a melee charachter would not be able to.  Endgame is a mixed bag for
Rangers.  No two weapon and restricted in good close combat weapons
means it can take a while to grind through hordes of weak monsters,
where using arrows would be annoying, on the other hand expert in
divnination means magic mapping galore which saves TONS of time
mapping Genhomme.

Knight's are considerably different.  They come stock with the ability
to jump (a knights move) and the same +1 long sword that valk's get
for early excaliber.  Knights more then make up for the lower starting
Dexterity of the other beat stick classes by the increased movement
capabilities offered by the horse, and clever use of the Lance.  Once
you've got a few levels under your bet, and let your poney grow, you
become a veritable speed demon when mounted.  A horse matches a player
in speed boots, a warhorse is faster still and when you hit your mount
with a wand of speed monster...  The lance should also not be
forgotten.  Jousting can be incredibly powerful for dueling monsters
due to its stun, as an expert in lance can lock down annoying covetous
monsters like Liches.  Did I mention that magic mirror enchanted magic
missile is powerful enough to make Wizard's blush?

So while it might be easier for a beginer to start playing with a Valk
or a Barbarian, what I'm essentially arguing is that by the time a
player has the skill to potentially pull off an ascention, Ranger or
Knight will give the starting player a better chance of pulling it
off- because their starting kits have more tactical flexibilty built
into them.

In Slash'EM... Rogue is by far the easiest class, strong starting
gear, the best weapon in the game as a guarenteed first sacrafice gift
(bat from hell makes grayswandir pale with its flat +20 damage), and a
variety of useful techniques for offing monsters in one hit.

I'm a bit torn on how to rank magic classes.  Wizards are a lot of fun
in both, Nethack and Slash'em, but I don't like running them like weak
Rangers (because why not just play 'R' or 'r').  I ususally try to zap
things with magic, and as a result have lower AC, and quickly run out
of power!  That said, despite having a more challenging early game,
once lategame hits Magic charachters really start to shine.  Of
course, early game is the most difficult part of ascending and for
that reason I wouldn't classify wizard as being easy (unless of course
you roll like a fiend for starting inventory...)


